WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.235
An expression describes a quantity without necessarily asserting an equation to solve

00:00:05.235 --> 00:00:14.100
Two copies of the quantity x plus three means that both the variable part and the three additional units are repeated twice.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.402
Distribution accounts for those repeated parts separately

00:00:18.402 --> 00:00:24.405
Two copies of x contribute two x, and two copies of three contribute six

00:00:24.405 --> 00:00:31.267
The rewritten expression gives the same output for every real input, as the overlapping graphs illustrate.

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:39.374
Like terms count the same variable quantity, so two x plus another x becomes three x

00:00:39.374 --> 00:00:41.926
The six remains a separate constant

00:00:41.926 --> 00:00:48.733
Adding coefficients is justified because the repeated units are identical, not because all symbols look similar.

00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:57.678
A leading minus means multiplication by negative one, and it affects every term inside the parentheses

00:00:57.678 --> 00:01:01.022
The two sides again represent one function

00:01:01.022 --> 00:01:07.233
Simplifying preserves all inputs; solving for a particular input requires an additional condition.
